{"placeholder":"<p>The vanishing gradient problem occurs when gradients are multiplied backwards through many layers (or time steps in RNNs) and shrink towards zero, making it extremely difficult for the network to learn long-range dependencies. Early layers or earlier time steps receive almost no useful gradient signal, so the network effectively 'forgets' information from the distant past. This is a major reason why plain RNNs struggle with long sequences and why architectures like LSTMs were introduced to mitigate it.</p>

<p>On the NeuralBytes channel, this topic is covered in the video <strong>"RNNs and LSTMs: Sequence Modeling Basics"</strong>, where the vanishing gradient problem is discussed in the context of recurrent neural networks and how LSTMs help solve it.</p>
